# Unlink the accounts deleted before backend plan 0232 (section 7). Core and
# catalog still hold rows of people that auth no longer knows: their profiles,
# voice recordings, app state, rows on other people's baskets, and their user
# id on every line, comment, purchase and shop walk. post.sh runs
# `node reconcile-deletions.js` inside the core pod and then the catalog pod.
# Each collects the user ids of its tables, asks auth which ones still exist,
# and runs the deletion of plan 0232 for every id auth does not know: the same
# deletion a `user.deleted` on the stream runs.
#
# THIS TASK IS ARMED FOR STAGING AND FOR PRODUCTION. It ships in the release
# that adds `reconcile-deletions.js` and `auth.existingUserIds`, so check.sh
# passes while the pods do not run that command yet, and post.sh, which runs
# after the rollout, counts again and refuses above the ceiling. A staging
# deploy until the first date below runs it. In production, release 0.16.0
# runs it, and only until the second date. No other version runs it there. A
# deploy after a date records the task as `expired`, which is final.
#
# A window is at most 14 days after the commit that writes it, and both are at
# that limit for a commit on 2026-10-29. If release 0.16.0 is published after
# 2026-11-12, move the dates in a new commit BEFORE that release, and move
# PRODUCTION_RELEASE with them if the version changed. A date that is moved
# after it passed does not revive a task that a deploy already recorded as
# expired.
# The deploy unit whose release runs this task (k8s plan 0015 D9).
UNIT="velista"
RUN_UNTIL_STAGING="2026-11-12"
RUN_UNTIL_PRODUCTION="2026-11-12"
PRODUCTION_RELEASE="0.16.0"
DATABASES="core catalog"
#
# The most ids, per service, that auth may not know for this task to start.
# check.sh and post.sh count them with `--count` and refuse above this number,
# and post.sh passes it to the command as `--max`, which refuses too.
#
# NEITHER CLUSTER WAS READ WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN. A deleted account leaves its
# id in a KICKED membership and on what it wrote, and every account the orphan
# reaper of plan 0011 deleted leaves its id in a `user.deleted:<id>` key of
# core's `processed_events`. That second kind is the larger one wherever many
# guests tried the app and left. 2000 is a guess on the high side for both
# clusters. A cluster above it is a question for a person, and the check phase
# of a deploy prints the count it found. Raise the number in a commit after
# reading that count, never to get past it.
MAX_UNKNOWN_IDS_STAGING="2000"
MAX_UNKNOWN_IDS_PRODUCTION="2000"
